Christopher J Burian <cburian@ux4.cso.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>Carl Kadie writes:
>]jmyers@mksol.dseg.ti.com (James E Myers) writes:

>]> I strongly believe that cracking or using another account is theft of
>]> University property and should be prosecuted as theft [...]
>
>]Yikes, why does everyone want to define everything as "theft". 
>
>Because "theft" is taken very, very seriously by the courts.  [...] 

One could make a fairly good argument that unauthorized use of a given 
computer system constitutes "theft of service."  This would be especially 
true on pay-for-access systems.  As far as the typical university system 
goes, I think it's another of those case-by-case situations.  At one time, 
we had a commercial software package, whose license cost was based on the 
CPU time burned up in its use.  When folks started coming in under their 
friends' passwords and using it, we paid real money; in that situation, I
would think that a charge of theft would be justified.  I wouldn't invoke
it unless we were talking about felony levels or repeated violations, how-
ever; I can't see filing charges for $5 in CPU time...

>The "theft" classification is an enormously powerful tool administrators 
>can arm themselves with, and they like the idea of that.

Actually, many of us don't like it one bit.  As I said, I (for one) would
not file such charges unless the individual went beyond the monetary level
of petty theft.  Of course, this is all moot if no money is changing hands;
I can't see a non-chargeback computing center claiming 'theft' if *no one*
pays for access/use.  In those cases, I'd think that 'misuse of services'
is the worst one could claim.

--Wes

