Whoah.  None of the discussion here has regarded any "centralized
authority" that's any more centralized than "the central system
administrator and/or owner of a particular site."

> What if the Nazi's only decided to censor Jews on there hit lists
> *WHEN THEY WERE ACTUALLY IN VIOLATION* Then this becomes ligitimate,
> however highly discriminitory, behavior.

(Note: "complain about" != "censor.")  What you're basically describing
is a scenario in which the Nazis are acting more like public-spirited
citizens -- _actively_ public-spirited citizens, anyway -- than, on
average, are the non-Nazis, in that the Nazis are working a lot harder
than everyone else to do something about net.misbehavior by some
people... I'd categorize this as an example of a good deed being done by
bad people for bad reasons -- like it or not, the _deed_ itself is still
good.

> I think an easier solution is to be more tolerent of things said in
> public forums, and ignore or killfile what you don't like, or create
> your own property where you can be a control freak to your hearts
> content.

Matter of opinion... basically, I learned in my very early youth two
things about bullies: (1) I don't like them and (2) the only way to make
them stop their bullying activities is through the application of
force sufficient to intimidate them into at least pretending to be 
civilized human beings.  Try to reason with them, and you just fuel
their contempt and sadism.  Try to ignore them, and the best you can
hope for is that they'll go find somebody else to torment.  But if you
*hurt* them, maybe, just maybe, they'll get it through their tiny
reptilian brains that tormenting other people ---> pain.

In the context of the net, I equate the act of deliberately indulging in
alt.systax.tactical-like behavior with bullying, and I equate the act of
reporting such behavior to the person's system administrator --
snitching -- with applying force against them.

